{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Whoever kills a person and whoever touches a corpse</b> – Rabbi Meir says: The verse speaks of one who kills with an object that receives impurity, and the verse teaches you that a utensil defiles a person through contact with the dead body as if he had touched the corpse itself. Now might one say even if he shot an arrow at it and killed it? Scripture says: \"and whoever touches a corpse.\" The verse juxtaposes the killer to the one who touches — just as the one who touches is defiled through physical contact, so too the killer is defiled through physical contact. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 31:19). But his words are not acceptable to me, for if he killed with an object that receives impurity of wooden vessels, the person does not contract seven-day impurity and does not require sprinkling, because the utensil is a primary source of impurity (av tum'ah) and the person who is defiled from it is a secondary derivative (rishon). And if with an iron vessel, we have already learned (Pesachim 14a) concerning a sword that it is like a corpse itself, to contract seven-day impurity. And if the Rav held that since he touched the utensil while it was still joined to the corpse, it would defile him like the corpse itself with seven-day impurity — impurity through connection is rabbinic when it comes to the connection of utensils to a corpse, as is explicitly stated in tractate Nazir, in the chapter Three Kinds (Nazir 42a). And the language in the Sifrei is as follows: Rabbi Meir says, the verse speaks of one who kills with an object that receives impurity, which defiles through carrying (heset). Or might one say even if he shot an arrow at it and killed it? Scripture says, etc. And its explanation is that Rabbi Meir comes to derive from here that a corpse defiles through carrying (masa), and if he killed it with a hand-staff or with a spear, even if it has no iron, and carried it on them at the moment of death — it defiles him through carrying with seven-day impurity. And he did not intend by \"an object that receives impurity\" a receptacle-vessel, but rather an object through which a person receives impurity from the corpse itself, such as carrying. And the opening of the baraita there is: From where do we derive that it defiles through carrying? You say: it is a fortiori — if an animal carcass (nevelah), which is lighter, defiles through carrying, then a corpse, which is weightier, is it not logical that it defiles through carrying? But if there it is only evening impurity, then here too should it only be evening impurity? You say: its carrying is like its touching — in a case where touching produces seven-day impurity, carrying produces seven-day impurity; in a case where touching produces evening impurity, carrying produces evening impurity. Rabbi Meir says, etc. From here the explanation becomes clear in the sense we stated: the first Tanna derived carrying of a corpse from an a fortiori argument, and even for seven-day impurity, but he does not apply the principle of dayo, whereas Rabbi Meir derived it from this verse, for he does apply the principle of dayo, and it does not come through a fortiori reasoning, as is stated in Bava Kamma in the chapter How is the Rule (Bava Kamma 24a)." "<b>You and your captives</b> – That the captives must purify their garments that touched a corpse, and every garment and every leather utensil that was in their hands, according to the law of Israel, so that they not defile the people through their garments and their utensils:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The verse citation 'Pesachim 14' refers to Pesachim 14a; 'Bava Kamma 24' refers to Bava Kamma 24a. 'דיו' (dayo) is the principle that an a fortiori inference yields only the same degree as its source, not a greater one."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:01.038978805Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}